"""Book 5 pass 2 (11 Oct 2026): full worked solutions, Chapters 1-8. S[chapter][problem] = text.
Format: Idea, the numbers, Check, The usual mistake."""
S = {}
S[1] = {
1: "Idea: 64 symbols carry log₂64 = 6 bits each, and a root-raised-cosine pulse occupies the symbol rate times (1 + α). Rb = 10 × 10⁶ × 6 = 60 Mbit/s, and B = 10 MHz × 1.2 = 12 MHz. Check: the spectral efficiency is 60/12 = 5 bit/s/Hz, which is 6 bits per symbol reduced by the 20 % excess bandwidth. The usual mistake is to take 64 bits per symbol instead of its logarithm.",
2: "Idea: Shannon's formula C = B log₂(1 + SNR) needs the SNR as a ratio. 10 dB is 10, so C = 5 MHz × log₂11 = 5 × 3.46 = 17.3 Mbit/s. Check: at 10 dB the efficiency of 3.46 bit/s/Hz lies between 3 bits (SNR 7) and 4 bits (SNR 15). The usual mistake is to put 10 into the logarithm as decibels and also forget the 1, getting log₂10 = 3.32.",
3: "Idea: SNR = (Eb/N0)(Rb/B), so in decibels the spectral efficiency adds 10 log₁₀η. SNR = 8 + 10 log₁₀2 = 8 + 3.0 = 11.0 dB. Check: at 1 bit/s/Hz the SNR and Eb/N0 would be equal, and each doubling of efficiency adds 3 dB. The usual mistake is to subtract the 3 dB, confusing energy per bit with energy per symbol in the other direction.",
4: "Idea: at the Shannon limit η = log₂(1 + SNR), so SNR = 2^η − 1. SNR = 2⁶ − 1 = 63, 10 log₁₀63 = 18.0 dB. Check: the rule of 3 dB per bit/s/Hz at high SNR gives about 6 × 3 = 18 dB. The usual mistake is to forget the −1 and quote 64, 18.06 dB, which is close here but wrong at low efficiency.",
5: "Idea: as the spectral efficiency goes to zero, the minimum Eb/N0 = (2^η − 1)/η tends to ln 2. Eb/N0 = 0.693, 10 log₁₀0.693 = −1.59 dB. Check: at η = 0.01 the formula gives (2^0.01 − 1)/0.01 = 0.695, already within 0.3 % of the limit. The usual mistake is to answer 0 dB, the limit at 1 bit/s/Hz.",
6: "Idea: compare how bandwidth and power enter C = B log₂(1 + SNR). Capacity is proportional to bandwidth but grows only with the logarithm of SNR, so at high SNR doubling the capacity through power alone means roughly squaring the SNR, while doubling the bandwidth doubles it at once. Check: at 20 dB, 6.66 bit/s/Hz, doubling to 13.3 bit/s/Hz needs about 40 dB, 100 times more power, although doubling the bandwidth also doubles the noise and so needs twice the power to keep the SNR. The usual mistake is to ignore that last point and claim bandwidth is free.",
7: "Idea: Carson's rule B ≈ 2(Δf + fm). B = 2 × (2.5 + 3) = 11 kHz. Check: it fits a 12.5 kHz channel with 1.5 kHz of guard, which is why narrowband land-mobile FM uses 2.5 kHz deviation. The usual mistake is to use the deviation alone, 5 kHz, and forget the audio bandwidth.",
8: "Idea: in AM the carrier carries power 1 and the two sidebands m²/2 together, so the sideband fraction is m²/(2 + m²). 0.25/2.25 = 0.111, 11 %. Check: even at m = 1 the fraction is only 1/3, which is why AM wastes most of its power on the carrier. The usual mistake is to use m instead of m², giving 20 %.",
9: "Idea: PAM4 carries 2 bits per symbol, and the Nyquist frequency is half the symbol rate. 56 Gbit/s/2 = 28 GBd, Nyquist frequency 14 GHz. Check: NRZ at 56 Gbit/s would need 28 GHz, twice as much, which is why PAM4 replaced it. The usual mistake is to set the Nyquist frequency equal to the symbol rate.",
10: "Idea: as bandwidth grows without limit, C approaches (S/N0)/ln 2 = 1.44 S/N0. 50 dB-Hz is 10⁵ Hz, so C = 1.44 × 10⁵ = 144 kbit/s. Check: Figure 1.1's 40 dB-Hz gave 14.4 kbit/s, and 10 dB more gives ten times more. The usual mistake is to treat dB-Hz as decibels of a power and convert with 20 log.",
}
S[2] = {
1: "Idea: a tone above fs/2 folds to |f − k fs|, the nearest image in the first Nyquist zone. 130 − 100 = 30 MHz, below fs/2 = 50 MHz, so it appears at 30 MHz. Check: a real sampler cannot tell 30 MHz from 130 MHz, since cos(2π × 130t) and cos(2π × 30t) agree at every t = n/100 µs. The usual mistake is to subtract from fs/2, giving 20 MHz.",
2: "Idea: an ideal N-bit converter with a full-scale sine has SQNR = 6.02N + 1.76 dB. 6.02 × 14 + 1.76 = 86.0 dB. Check: each bit adds about 6 dB, and 14 bits × 6 = 84 plus 1.76 is close. The usual mistake is to omit the 1.76 dB, which comes from the sine's 3 dB crest factor relative to a uniform error.",
3: "Idea: invert SQNR = 6.02N + 1.76 to get the effective bits. ENOB = (74 − 1.76)/6.02 = 12.0 bits. Check: 12 bits ideal gives 74.0 dB. The usual mistake is to divide 74 by 6.02 without subtracting 1.76, giving 12.3 bits.",
4: "Idea: noise spreads over the sample bandwidth, and filtering to the channel removes the rest. Processing gain = 10 log₁₀(61.44/10) = 7.9 dB. Check: 61.44/10 is a factor of about 6, and a factor of 4 would be 6 dB, 8 would be 9 dB. The usual mistake is to use fs/2 for a complex converter, whose noise spans the full fs.",
5: "Idea: jitter limits SNR to −20 log₁₀(2π f σt). 2π × 10⁹ × 10⁻¹³ = 6.28 × 10⁻⁴, and −20 log₁₀ of that is 64.0 dB. Check: halving the jitter adds 6 dB, so 50 fs would give 70 dB, which is the class of clock that 1 GHz sampling needs. The usual mistake is to use 10 log, giving 32 dB.",
6: "Idea: count the independent real numbers per second. Each complex sample is two real numbers, so fs complex samples per second describe a band from −fs/2 to +fs/2 without ambiguity; a real signal's spectrum is symmetric about zero, so a real sampler's fs numbers describe only 0 to fs/2. Check: both carry fs × 2 or fs real values per second and cover fs or fs/2 of bandwidth, the same ratio. The usual mistake is to think the complex sampler breaks Nyquist's rule; it uses two converters.",
7: "Idea: for bandpass sampling with zone index n, 2fH/n ≤ fs ≤ 2fL/(n − 1). For n = 2: 2 × 160/2 = 160 MS/s to 2 × 140/1 = 280 MS/s. Check: at 200 MS/s the band 140-160 MHz lies in the second zone, 100-200 MHz, and folds to 40-60 MHz without overlap. The usual mistake is to swap fL and fH, which makes the range empty.",
8: "Idea: the 6.02N + 1.76 figure assumes a full-scale sine, whose RMS sits 3 dB below full scale; every extra decibel of back-off costs a decibel. 13 − 3 = 10 dB of loss, so 86.0 − 10 = 76.0 dB. Check: an OFDM signal with a 13 dB crest factor needs that back-off, which costs it about 1.7 bits. The usual mistake is to subtract the full 13 dB.",
9: "Idea: in a zero-IF radio, DC offset and LO leakage land at 0 Hz in baseband, the center subcarrier. Leaving it empty keeps that error off the data, at the cost of one subcarrier of many. Check: a 64-point Wi-Fi symbol loses 1/64 of its subcarriers, 1.6 %. The usual mistake is to blame the center subcarrier's frequency for some propagation effect.",
10: "Idea: an N-bit accumulator divides the clock into 2^N steps. 245.76 × 10⁶/2^N < 0.1 requires 2^N > 2.46 × 10⁹; 2³¹ = 2.15 × 10⁹ is too small, so N = 32, with 0.057 Hz resolution. Check: 245.76 × 10⁶/4.29 × 10⁹ = 0.057 Hz. The usual mistake is to stop at 31 bits after a rough logarithm.",
}
S[3] = {
1: "Idea: occupied bandwidth = symbol rate × (1 + α). 5 × 1.22 = 6.1 MHz. Check: a 22 % roll-off spreads the 5 MHz Nyquist bandwidth by 1.1 MHz. The usual mistake is to use half the symbol rate, the one-sided baseband width.",
2: "Idea: invert B = Rs(1 + α). 6/1.15 = 5.22 Msymbol/s. Check: 5.22 × 1.15 = 6.0 MHz. The usual mistake is to subtract 15 % from 6 MHz, giving 5.1 Msymbol/s.",
3: "Idea: two requirements must hold at once, a matched receive filter and an overall Nyquist pulse. Splitting the raised cosine into two root-raised-cosine halves meets both, because the receive filter is then the time-reversed transmit pulse and their product is the full raised cosine. Check: a full raised cosine at the transmitter would need a flat receive filter, which passes extra noise, or more filtering, which reintroduces interference. The usual mistake is to think the split is only for implementation convenience.",
4: "Idea: a matched filter's peak SNR is 2E/N0. 2 × 10⁻⁹/(2 × 10⁻¹²) = 1000, 30.0 dB. Check: E/N0 alone is 500, 27 dB, and the matched filter doubles it in this convention. The usual mistake is to use E/N0 and report 27 dB.",
5: "Idea: the eye's width is time, its height is amplitude. Timing jitter closes it horizontally; noise closes it vertically. Check: an eye with ideal amplitude but 0.3 symbols of peak jitter loses 30 % of its width. The usual mistake is to blame noise for all eye closure.",
6: "Idea: read the worst-case interference values for the two roll-offs at τ = 0.1 T from Section 3.2. 0.510/0.197 = 2.6 times. Check: smaller α means slower-decaying tails, so more interference from timing errors is expected. The usual mistake is to invert the ratio and conclude the narrower pulse is more tolerant.",
7: "Idea: B = Rs(1 + α). 3.84 × 1.22 = 4.68 MHz, inside the 5 MHz channel. Check: the 0.32 MHz left over is the guard band between WCDMA carriers. The usual mistake is to forget α and quote 3.84 MHz.",
8: "Idea: only the cascade of the two root filters is a Nyquist pulse. A full raised cosine after a root transmit filter gives a combined response that is not Nyquist, so intersymbol interference remains, and the receive filter is no longer matched to the pulse. Check: the combined response would be RC × RRC, which does not cross zero at the symbol instants. The usual mistake is to assume more filtering always helps.",
9: "Idea: third-order products of a band of width W reach out to three times W. 3 × 20 = 60 MHz, centered on the signal. Check: the edges of the 20 MHz signal mix as 2f1 − f2 to land 20 MHz beyond each edge. The usual mistake is to double the bandwidth, which is the second-order case.",
10: "Idea: an LTE resource block is 12 × 15 kHz = 180 kHz. 50 × 180 kHz = 9 MHz, 90 % of the 10 MHz channel. Check: the remaining 1 MHz is the guard band. The usual mistake is to count 10 MHz/15 kHz = 667 subcarriers instead of the 600 actually used.",
}
S[4] = {
1: "Idea: BPSK error rate is Q(√(2Eb/N0)). 6.8 dB is 4.786, so √9.57 = 3.094, and Q(3.094) = 9.9 × 10⁻⁴. Check: the familiar point 10⁻³ at 6.8 dB. The usual mistake is to use Q(√(Eb/N0)), which gives 1.4 × 10⁻².",
2: "Idea: read the required Es/N0 for 64-QAM and 256-QAM from Section 4.2's table at the same symbol error rate. 30.08 − 23.97 = 6.1 dB. Check: each step of two bits per symbol in square QAM costs about 6 dB. The usual mistake is to expect 3 dB per step.",
3: "Idea: compare the operating Es/N0 with the required value. 26 dB exceeds the 23.97 dB needed for 10⁻³, so the error rate is below 10⁻³. Check: 2 dB of margin on a steep waterfall curve lowers the error rate by more than an order of magnitude. The usual mistake is to compare against the bit-error requirement in Eb/N0.",
4: "Idea: EVM is an amplitude ratio, so 20 log₁₀. 20 log₁₀0.02 = −34.0 dB. Check: 1 % is −40 dB, and doubling adds 6 dB. The usual mistake is 10 log, −17 dB.",
5: "Idea: PAPR of a constellation = corner point power/average power. Corner (3, 3) gives 18; the average of 1² + 1², 1² + 3², 3² + 3² over equally likely points is 10. 18/10 = 1.8, 2.55 dB. Check: 64-QAM's ratio is 98/42 = 2.33, larger as expected. The usual mistake is to use the outer amplitude only, 3, against an average amplitude.",
6: "Idea: QPSK is two BPSK signals on orthogonal carriers. Each carries one bit with half the symbol energy, so Eb, the noise per dimension, and each decision are identical to BPSK, and the bit error rate is the same. Check: the symbol error rate of QPSK is about twice the bit error rate because a symbol fails if either bit does. The usual mistake is to compare at equal Es/N0, where QPSK is 3 dB worse.",
7: "Idea: with Gray coding, most symbol errors go to a neighbor and flip one of the log₂M bits. BER ≈ 6 × 10⁻⁴/6 = 1 × 10⁻⁴. Check: 64-QAM has 6 bits per symbol. The usual mistake is to take BER equal to SER.",
8: "Idea: small phase noise of θ radians gives an error vector of length θ relative to the point. 0.3° = 5.24 mrad, 20 log₁₀(5.24 × 10⁻³) = −45.6 dB, 0.52 %. Check: 1° gives 1.75 % and −35 dB. The usual mistake is to use degrees directly.",
9: "Idea: independent impairments add in power. 10^(−3.8) + 10^(−4.2) + 10^(−4.4) = 1.585 × 10⁻⁴ + 0.631 × 10⁻⁴ + 0.398 × 10⁻⁴ = 2.61 × 10⁻⁴, −35.8 dB. Check: the total is worse than the worst single term by 2.2 dB. The usual mistake is to add the decibel values or the percentages.",
10: "Idea: corner power over average. Corner 2 × 7² = 98; average 42. 98/42 = 2.33, 3.68 dB. Check: larger than 16-QAM's 2.55 dB, approaching the 4.77 dB limit of large square QAM. The usual mistake is to take the average as 2 × 4² = 32.",
}
S[5] = {
1: "Idea: coherence bandwidth ≈ 1/(5 στ) for 0.9 correlation. 1/(5 × 1 µs) = 200 kHz. Check: a 200 kHz GSM carrier sees flat fading in such a channel, a 5 MHz LTE carrier does not. The usual mistake is to use 1/στ, 1 MHz.",
2: "Idea: fD = v f/c. 60 km/h = 16.7 m/s, so fD = 16.7 × 5.9 × 10⁹/2.998 × 10⁸ = 328 Hz. Check: at 5.9 GHz the wavelength is 5.1 cm, and 16.7/0.051 = 328 Hz. The usual mistake is to leave the speed in km/h.",
3: "Idea: Tc ≈ 0.423/fD. 0.423/328 = 1.29 ms. Check: a vehicle moving half a wavelength, 2.5 cm, takes 1.5 ms at 16.7 m/s, the same order. The usual mistake is to use 1/fD, 3.0 ms, which overstates the coherence.",
4: "Idea: Rayleigh BPSK error rate ≈ 1/(4γ) at high SNR. γ = 1/(4 × 10⁻⁴) = 2500, 34.0 dB. Check: the AWGN channel needs 8.4 dB for 10⁻⁴, so fading costs about 26 dB. The usual mistake is to use 1/(2γ).",
5: "Idea: read the required SNR per branch from Section 5.4 for one and two branches. 24.0 − 11.1 = 12.9 dB less. Check: diversity changes the slope of the error curve, so the saving is far larger than the 3 dB of array gain. The usual mistake is to answer 3 dB.",
6: "Idea: compare the signal bandwidth with the coherence bandwidth. Indoors the coherence bandwidth is a few megahertz; a 20 MHz signal spans several and sees notches across its band, while a 300 kHz signal sits inside one and fades as a whole. Check: an RMS delay spread of 50 ns gives 1/(5 × 50 ns) = 4 MHz. The usual mistake is to think frequency selectivity depends on the carrier frequency.",
7: "Idea: path loss beyond the reference grows as 10n log₁₀(d2/d1). 10 × 4.5 × log₁₀3 = 21.5 dB. Check: free space, n = 2, would give 9.5 dB. The usual mistake is to use 20 log, 43 dB.",
8: "Idea: for 95 % coverage at the cell edge, the margin is the one-sided Gaussian point 1.64σ. 1.64 × 6 = 9.9 dB. Check: 90 % would need 1.28σ, 7.7 dB. The usual mistake is to use 1.96σ, the two-sided 95 % point.",
9: "Idea: a Rayleigh signal falls below a fraction x of its mean power with probability 1 − e^(−x) ≈ x. x = 10⁻³, a margin of 30 dB. Check: each factor of ten in outage costs 10 dB in Rayleigh fading. The usual mistake is to use a Gaussian margin, which applies to shadowing, not fast fading.",
10: "Idea: Bc ≈ 1/(5 στ). 1/(5 × 300 ns) = 667 kHz, and 100 MHz/667 kHz ≈ 150 coherence bandwidths. Check: 5G NR at 30 kHz spacing has 3276 subcarriers, about 22 per coherence bandwidth, so the channel is nearly flat across each subcarrier. The usual mistake is to use 1/στ, giving 3.3 MHz and 30.",
}
S[6] = {
1: "Idea: Δf = fs/N, and the useful symbol time T = 1/Δf. 15.36 MHz/1024 = 15 kHz, T = 66.7 µs. Check: LTE's spacing is 15 kHz with a 66.7 µs symbol. The usual mistake is to divide the FFT size by the sample rate.",
2: "Idea: overhead = guard/(useful + guard). 3.2/(12.8 + 3.2) = 20 %. Check: the Wi-Fi 6 long guard costs 20 %, the 0.8 µs guard 5.9 %. The usual mistake is to divide by the useful time alone, 25 %.",
3: "Idea: the prefix absorbs echoes delayed up to its length, a path difference of c × Tcp. 4.69 µs × 2.998 × 10⁸ = 1.41 km. Check: 1 µs is 300 m, and 4.69 × 300 = 1.41 km. The usual mistake is to halve it as if it were a round trip.",
4: "Idea: µ = 2 means 15 × 2² kHz. 60 kHz, T = 16.7 µs, and 14 symbols per slot give 0.25 ms. Check: each step of µ halves the slot from 1 ms. The usual mistake is to use 2µ instead of 2^µ.",
5: "Idea: N subcarriers in phase add to N times the average power. 10 log₁₀256 = 24.1 dB. Check: real signals reach this only with vanishing probability; 10⁻⁴ clipping occurs near 11-12 dB. The usual mistake is 20 log, 48 dB.",
6: "Idea: the cyclic prefix makes the channel a circular convolution over the FFT window. The FFT turns circular convolution into a separate complex gain on each subcarrier, which one multiplication by its inverse removes. Check: without the prefix, echoes spill between symbols and the gains are no longer independent. The usual mistake is to think OFDM needs no equalizer at all.",
7: "Idea: ICI ≈ (πε)²/3 for a normalized offset ε. (πε)²/3 = 10⁻⁴ gives ε = √(3 × 10⁻⁴)/π = 0.0055. Check: (π × 0.0055)²/3 = 1.0 × 10⁻⁴. The usual mistake is to forget the square root and quote 10⁻⁴/π.",
8: "Idea: convert ppm to hertz, then normalize. 0.2 ppm of 6 GHz is 1.2 kHz, ε = 1.2/78.125 = 0.0154, (πε)²/3 = 7.8 × 10⁻⁴, −31.1 dB. Check: the narrow Wi-Fi 7 spacing makes the same ppm four times worse in ε than at 312.5 kHz. The usual mistake is to use the 20 MHz channel bandwidth instead of the subcarrier spacing.",
9: "Idea: common phase error rotates every subcarrier of a symbol by the same angle. The pilots in that symbol measure the angle, and the equalizer removes it before decisions. Check: only the part of phase noise that varies within a symbol, which causes ICI, is left. The usual mistake is to treat all phase noise as irreducible.",
10: "Idea: redo Worked Example 6.5's two bounds at the new speed. 350 km/h = 97.2 m/s, Doppler 97.2 × 2 × 10⁹/3 × 10⁸ = 648 Hz, requiring a spacing of at least 648/0.02 = 32.4 kHz, above the 17 kHz cap set by the 4.7 µs echoes. The bounds cross: a high-speed train needs more prefix overhead or shorter echoes. Check: the Doppler is 350/120 = 2.92 times the 222 Hz of the example. The usual mistake is to pick 30 kHz and ignore that its 2.3 µs prefix is shorter than the echoes.",
}
S[7] = {
1: "Idea: information rate = coded rate × code rate. 40 × 3/4 = 30 Mbit/s. Check: the code adds one parity bit for every three information bits. The usual mistake is to divide by the code rate, 53 Mbit/s.",
2: "Idea: minimum Eb/N0 = (2^η − 1)/η. (2⁴ − 1)/4 = 3.75, 5.74 dB. Check: at 1 bit/s/Hz the answer is 0 dB, at 2 it is 1.76 dB, rising with η. The usual mistake is to give the SNR, 15 = 11.8 dB.",
3: "Idea: two equal orthogonal streams split the power, each at half the SNR. 30 dB = 1000, so 2 log₂(1 + 500) = 17.9 bit/s/Hz, against log₂1001 = 9.97 bit/s/Hz for one antenna each side. Check: the ratio is 1.8, approaching 2 at high SNR. The usual mistake is to give each stream the full SNR.",
4: "Idea: rate = data subcarriers × bits per subcarrier × code rate/symbol time. 234 × 10 × 5/6/13.6 µs = 1950 bits/13.6 µs = 143.4 Mbit/s. Check: this is the published Wi-Fi 6 MCS 11 rate for 20 MHz with a 0.8 µs guard. The usual mistake is to use the 12.8 µs useful time, 152 Mbit/s.",
5: "Idea: spatial streams are limited by the smaller antenna count. min(4, 2) = 2. Check: the channel matrix is 2 × 4 or 4 × 2 and has rank at most 2. The usual mistake is to answer 4.",
6: "Idea: receive combining raises one stream's SNR; spatial multiplexing adds streams. A 1 × 4 link gains up to 6 dB, about two more bits per second per hertz, while a 4 × 4 link carries four streams and multiplies the capacity by about four. Check: at 20 dB, log₂(1 + 400) = 8.6 against 4 × log₂26 = 18.8. The usual mistake is to think more antennas always multiply capacity.",
7: "Idea: four equal orthogonal paths split the SNR four ways. 20 dB = 100, so 4 log₂(1 + 25) = 18.80 bit/s/Hz, against log₂101 = 6.66 for SISO, a ratio of 2.8. Check: at higher SNR the ratio approaches 4. The usual mistake is to give each path the full 100.",
8: "Idea: a convolutional encoder's state is its last K − 1 inputs. 2⁴ = 16 states. Check: K = 7 gives 64, the Wi-Fi code. The usual mistake is 2⁵.",
9: "Idea: the transmitter does not know the channel, so it divides its power between the two antennas, while a receive combiner collects the full power from both. That halving is the 3 dB difference; the diversity order, two, is the same. Check: at high SNR both curves have the same slope, offset by 3 dB. The usual mistake is to think Alamouti has less diversity.",
10: "Idea: decibels of gain convert to a power ratio by 10^(dB/10). 10^0.7 = 5.0 times. Check: 6 dB is 4 and 7 dB a little more. The usual mistake is 10^(7/20) = 2.2.",
}
S[8] = {
1: "Idea: the 2.4 GHz band is 83.5 MHz wide and channels are 5 MHz apart, so 20 MHz channels overlap unless separated by five steps. Channels 1, 6, and 11 fit without overlap, three in all. Check: 2412, 2437, and 2462 MHz centers, 25 MHz apart. The usual mistake is 83.5/20 = 4.",
2: "Idea: divide the band by the channel width. 1200/80 = 15. Check: the 6 GHz band, 5925-7125 MHz, has 14 usable 80 MHz channels in the 802.11 plan because of its edges; 15 is the arithmetic ceiling. The usual mistake is to use 160 MHz channels.",
3: "Idea: recall the 3GPP ranges. FR1 runs from 410 MHz to 7.125 GHz, FR2 from 24.25 to 71 GHz. Check: FR2 was extended from 52.6 GHz to 71 GHz in Release 17. The usual mistake is to stop FR1 at 6 GHz.",
4: "Idea: floor = −174 + 10 log₁₀B + NF. 10 log₁₀(160 × 10⁶) = 82.0, so −174 + 82.0 + 5 = −87.0 dBm. Check: a 20 MHz channel is 9 dB lower, −96 dBm. The usual mistake is to forget the noise figure.",
5: "Idea: sensitivity = noise floor + required SNR. −87.0 + 30 = −57.0 dBm. Check: a 20 MHz channel with the same noise figure and SNR would reach 9 dB lower, −66 dBm. The usual mistake is to subtract the SNR.",
6: "Idea: identify where impairments land. The zero-IF receiver mixes the wanted signal directly to DC, where offsets and flicker noise sit, and relies on matched I and Q arms to reject the image at the mirror frequency, so it is the most sensitive to both. Check: a superheterodyne's IF is far from DC and its image is removed by a filter. The usual mistake is to name the superheterodyne because of its image problem.",
7: "Idea: data rate = sample rate × bytes per sample. 16-bit I and Q are 4 bytes, so 61.44 × 10⁶ × 4 = 245.8 MB/s. Check: that is 1.97 Gbit/s, beyond USB 2.0 and within USB 3.0. The usual mistake is to count 2 bytes per complex sample.",
8: "Idea: the strong signal controls the front end. It sets the gain and fills the 8-bit converter's full scale, leaving about 50 dB of dynamic range below it, and the tuner's limited linearity adds intermodulation on top of the wanted signal. Check: an 8-bit converter's ideal SQNR is 49.9 dB. The usual mistake is to blame the image response alone.",
9: "Idea: a single-pole RC corner is 1/(2πτ). τ = 75 µs, f = 1/(2π × 75 µs) = 2.12 kHz. Check: Europe's 50 µs gives 3.18 kHz. The usual mistake is to give 1/τ = 13.3 kHz.",
}
S[8][10] = "Idea: the transmitter's noise in the receive band adds to the receiver's own floor, and a 1 dB rise allows added noise 10 log₁₀(10^0.1 − 1) = −5.9 dB relative to that floor. The floor is −174 + 5 = −169 dBm/Hz, so the added noise may reach −174.9 dBm/Hz, and the isolation must be −130 − (−174.9) = 44.9 dB. Check: Worked Example 8.6 allowed only 0.5 dB of desensitization, which costs 9.1 dB relative to the floor instead of 5.9, and needed 51.1 dB with 2 dB more amplifier noise and 1 dB less noise figure: 51.1 − 2 − 1 − 3.2 = 44.9 dB. The usual mistake is to set the added noise equal to the floor, which costs 3 dB of sensitivity."
